WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.868
An eigenvector is a nonzero vector whose image is a scalar multiple of itself

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:08.993
For this matrix, one, one becomes three, three

00:00:08.993 --> 00:00:15.633
The arrow grows but stays on the same line, so it is an eigenvector with eigenvalue three.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:25.595
To find every eigenvector for eigenvalue three, solve the homogeneous system formed by A minus three times the identity

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:28.786
Both equations reduce to y equals x

00:00:28.786 --> 00:00:33.800
Therefore every nonzero multiple of one, one belongs to this eigendirection.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.349
There is another eigendirection: one, negative one remains unchanged, giving eigenvalue one

00:00:41.349 --> 00:00:46.035
Two independent eigenvectors form a useful coordinate system here

00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:54.367
Along these directions the same transformation acts by separate scalar multipliers rather than mixed coordinate formulas.

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:59.882
Decompose two, zero into one copy of each eigendirection

00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:04.567
Linearity lets us transform those pieces separately, then recombine them

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.909
The result is four, two

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:13.467
This simplification explains why an eigenvector basis is powerful when such a basis exists.
